From kenneth.scheve at yale.edu  Mon Jul 28 09:03:52 2008
From: kenneth.scheve at yale.edu (Kenneth Scheve)
Date: Mon, 28 Jul 2008 09:03:52 -0400
Subject: [amelia] Hausman test with "miest" command in STATA?
In-Reply-To: <30615.92764.qm@web46113.mail.sp1.yahoo.com>
References: <30615.92764.qm@web46113.mail.sp1.yahoo.com>
Message-ID: <488DC3B8.8000903@yale.edu>


So unfortunately miest does not do what you want without a little Stata 
programming. I don't think however it would be very difficult. The 
procedure would go something like this:

1. Estimate model with fixed effects using miest. This procedure allows 
you to save the vector of parameter estimates and the variance 
covariance matrix (_mib and _miVCE respectively). You need to names 
these and save them.

2. Estimate model without fixed effects using miest. Save the parameter 
estimates and variance covariance matrix with of course different names 
from Step #1.

3. Select the relevant parameters and parts of the variance-covariance 
matrix from Step #1 so they match those in Step #2 (that is drop all the 
fixed effects if you are estimating fixed effects by the LSDV method, if 
not this step shouldn't be necessary as the parameter vectors and varcov 
matrices will be of the same dimension and for the same parameters).

4. Conduct Hausman test on your two sets of parameter and varcov 
matrices using the standard formulas. This will have to be done using 
Stata's matrix functions but the formula is simple so it should not be 
too hard.

I know this not quite as easy or quick as you hoped but I think it is 
pretty straightforward.

Ken

